---
title: Site search
summary: Add one component to your layout, and every build indexes your site with Pagefind for a search dialog that works on any host.
---

Search is free and static. Once a page uses `<Search>`, every `astro build` indexes your pages with [Pagefind](https://pagefind.app) and writes the index into `dist/_vesper/search/`. It's plain files, so there's no service to run, and no MIME type or rewrite to set on the host.

## Add search to your layout

```astro
---
import { Search, SearchHead } from 'vespercms/components';
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <SearchHead />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <Search />
    </header>
    <main>
      <slot />
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
```

- `<Search />` renders a **Search** button that opens a dialog with the field, filters and results.
- `<SearchHead />` goes in `<head>`. It links the site's `opensearch.xml`, so browsers can offer your search from the address bar. It needs Astro's `site` setting, and renders nothing without it.

There's no other step: no command to run and no change to how you deploy. Until a page uses `<Search>`, the build writes no index and no `/search` page, and its log says so.

## Using the dialog

- **Ctrl+K** (⌘K on a Mac) opens it from anywhere on the page, and the button's description tells screen reader users so. There's no single-key shortcut such as "/", because WCAG requires that visitors can turn one off; a shortcut with Ctrl or ⌘ needs no switch.
- Results appear as you type, and the count is announced once typing pauses: "12 results for “nursing”".
- Down Arrow moves from the field to the first result, and Up and Down Arrow move between results. Results come ten at a time; **Show more results** moves focus to the first new one.
- Escape closes the dialog, and focus returns to the button. A click outside it closes it too.

Each result shows the page's title as its link, the entry's date, an excerpt with the matches marked, and the square crop of the entry's first image, so a focal point set in the studio carries through. Thumbnails need `sharp`, the package Astro's own image service uses.

| Prop | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `variant` | `'dialog'` | `'inline'` puts the field and results in the page instead. Use it on a search page. |
| `shortcut` | `true` | `false` turns off Ctrl+K. Only the first dialog on a page takes the shortcut. |
| `lang` | Astro's current locale, then `en` | The language of the words written into the page. |
| `id` | | Needed only for a second `<Search>` on one page. |
| `class` | | Added to the component's outer element. |

## Filters and sorting

Every entry's page has a **Section** filter: its collection's label. You can add your own filters, and fields to sort by, in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
vesper({
  search: {
    filters: { blog: ['tags', 'author'] },
    sort: { blog: ['publishedAt'] },
  },
});
```

- Keys are collection names, and values are field names from its schema, with a dotted path for a field inside an object (`seo.keywords`).
- **Filters** can be text, lists of text, selects and references. Each is a group of checkboxes under the field's label, showing a select's labels and a referenced entry's title.
- **Sort** fields can be dates, numbers and text. The Sort menu appears once a visitor picks a section that has them, because Pagefind leaves out pages with no value for the chosen field.
- A result's date is its first date field in `sort`, or else the entry's first date field.

The build warns about a field that doesn't exist or can't filter or sort, and leaves it out.

## What's indexed

Every built page, except:

- Astro's redirect pages, the studio at `/admin` and the 404 page
- the search page, and any page with `<Search variant="inline" />`
- pages with `<meta name="robots" content="noindex">`
- pages that match a pattern in `search.exclude`
- entries of a collection with `search: false` in `vesper.config.ts`
- entries hidden from search, below

```js
vesper({ search: { exclude: ['/tags/**'] } });
```

Patterns match the page's path without Astro's `base`. `*` matches within one part of the path, `**` across parts.

The index reads each page's `<main>`, so headers, menus and footers stay out of the excerpts. If your pages mark their own content with Pagefind's `data-pagefind-body`, Vesper uses that instead, and warns about the pages Pagefind then drops for not having it. Pagefind's `data-pagefind-ignore` works as usual. Vesper adds the entry's title, date and filters to the copy of each page it hands Pagefind; the HTML in `dist/` is never changed.

{% callout type="warning" %}
Use the inline variant only on your search page. Vesper leaves every page with an inline `<Search>` out of the index, so an inline search in a shared layout would hide every page built with that layout.
{% /callout %}

### Hide one entry

Add `fields.hideFromSearch()` to the collection's schema:

```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { fields } from 'vespercms/fields';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: '**/[^_]*.md', base: './src/content/blog' }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    hideFromSearch: fields.hideFromSearch(),
  }),
});
```

Editors get a **Hide from site search** switch in the entry's Details. The page stays on the site. The value is a plain `true` or `false` in the file, so your layout can also use it for `<meta name="robots">`. Without this field, Vesper uses a boolean `noindex` field if your schema has one, at the top level or under `seo`.

Vesper matches pages to entries through each collection's `url` in `vesper.config.ts`, so the switch and `search: false` apply only to collections with a `url`. See [Pages and URLs](/docs/content/pages-and-urls/).

## The /search page

When your pages use `<Search>`, Vesper adds a plain page at `/search`. It reads `?q=`, so a search can be linked and bookmarked, and asks search engines not to index it.

For a search page in your own layout, add `src/pages/search.astro`. Yours replaces Vesper's:

```astro
---
import { Search } from 'vespercms/components';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Search">
  <h1>Search</h1>
  <Search variant="inline" />
</Layout>
```

The inline variant keeps the query in the page's address as you type, so a reload or a shared link searches again.

## Without JavaScript

- The **Search** button is a link to `/search`.
- `/search` says that search needs JavaScript, and links to the home page, or to `search.noJsLink`. If the site uses `@astrojs/sitemap`, it links to the site map too.
- With `noJsFallback: 'duckduckgo'`, the form searches your site on DuckDuckGo instead, and says so. It needs Astro's `site`.

```js
vesper({ search: { noJsFallback: 'duckduckgo' } });
```

## Theming

`<Search>` uses your site's font and colours. Four custom properties do the rest:

```css
:root {
  --vesper-search-accent: #1f5fd1;  /* focus ring and the tint of matches; default: the system highlight */
  --vesper-search-surface: #ffffff; /* the dialog's background; default: Canvas */
  --vesper-search-border: #4d566b;  /* borders, at 3:1 against the surface; default: currentColor */
  --vesper-search-radius: 0.5rem;   /* default: 0.5rem */
}
```

In Windows high contrast and other forced-colour modes, matches use the system's own colours so they stay visible.

## Other languages

Pagefind keeps one index per `<html lang>`, so a multilingual site gets results in the page's own language. `<Search>`'s words are English. Give other languages with `search.strings`:

```js
vesper({
  search: {
    strings: {
      fr: { button: 'Rechercher', label: 'Rechercher sur ce site', close: 'Fermer' },
    },
  },
});
```

- Keys are lower-case language codes. A page in `fr-CA` uses the words under `fr-ca` if you give them, else those under `fr`. A word you leave out stays English.
- The button and the field's label are written into the page in the `lang` you pass to `<Search>`, which defaults to Astro's current locale. Without Astro's i18n routing, pass it yourself: `<Search lang="fr" />`.

The words you can replace:

- **The button and field:** `button`, `label`, `close`, `shortcut` (with `{key}`)
- **Results:** `results`, `oneResult` and `noResults` (with `{count}` and `{query}`), `resultsList`, `more`, `loadError`, `noIndexYet`
- **Sorting:** `sort`, `bestMatch`, and `newest`, `oldest`, `lowest`, `highest`, `aToZ`, `zToA` (each with `{field}`)
- **The search page:** `pageTitle`, `noJs`, `noJsHome`, `noJsLink`, `noJsSitemap`, `noJsDuckDuckGo`

## Size

The build log says how many pages were indexed, how large the index is, and what a first search loads. Pagefind loads the index in chunks as they're needed, so a large site stays fast. The build warns when a first search loads more than 300 KB, counted uncompressed, which is what a host without compression sends. If you see it, leave pages out with `exclude`, or turn on compression at your host.

## In astro dev

Search uses the index from your last build. Before the first build, it says "Search works after astro build."

## Turn search off

```js
vesper({ search: false });
```

The build then writes no index, no `/search` page and no `opensearch.xml`.
